{"name":"Soft Impala","hex":"#a28b7e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"soft","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Easily giving way under pressure.","Smooth and flexible; not rough, rugged, or harsh."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Be quiet; hold; stop; not so fast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soft"},{"term":"impala","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An African antelope, Aepyceros melampus, noted for its leaping ability; the male has ridged, curved horns."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/impala"}],"entries":[{"title":"Impala","blocks":[{"text":"The impala or rooibok is a medium-sized antelope found in eastern and southern Africa. The only extant member of the genus Aepyceros, and tribe Aepycerotini, it was first described to Europeans by German zoologist Hinrich Lichtenstein in 1812. Two subspecies are recognised—the grassland-dwelling common impala, and the larger and darker black-faced impala, which lives in slightly more arid, scrubland environments. The impala reaches 70–92 cm (28–36 in) at the shoulder and weighs 40–65 kg (88–143 lb). It features a glossy, reddish brown coat. The male's slender, lyre-shaped horns are 45–92 cm (18–36 in) long."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Common_impala_%28Aepyceros_melampus_melampus%29_male_Serengeti.jpg/330px-Common_impala_%28Aepyceros_melampus_melampus%29_male_Serengeti.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impala"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Impalas_%28Aepyceros_melampus%29_female_and_young_%2811421993164%29.jpg/500px-Impalas_%28Aepyceros_melampus%29_female_and_young_%2811421993164%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Impalas_%28Aepyceros_melampus%29_female_and_young